[Black "Introduction to The Burger Technique"]
[Result "*"]

 { Have you approached a position by immediately calculating the variations of the most tempting candidate moves, only to find out much later that you are barking up the wrong tree? Don’t worry, you are not alone. All too often, players fall victim to the lure of speed and are only too eager to calculate moves. As Mahatma Gandhi said “Speed is irrelevant if you are going in the wrong direction.” So, how do we know which tree to bark up? Before deciding which moves to work on, we need to first understand the position’s static features, namely material count and existing/potential threats. And in this chapter, I am going to introduce a systematic technique to do just that. I call it The Burger Technique. The burger is used as a metaphor to denote the different steps involved. In its simplest form, a burger has three layers. Likewise, this technique comprises three steps. They are as follows: _________________________________________________ 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance. For example, if you are down a Queen, you should be looking at mating attacks rather than gaining a piece or two. You can think of this as the topmost layer of the burger. 2. Identify the tactical motifs on the board. In layman terms, tactical motifs are essentially existing threats / potential threats, which cover a wide range of elements including undefended/hanging pieces, forkable pieces, and a vulnerable King position. When the correct existing/potential threats are identified, the best move will present itself. Because this is usually unfamiliar ground to most club players, we will devote the bulk of the chapter to this step. Think of this as the core of the burger. Just as the flavour of the burger exudes from this layer, the success of your calculation largely hinges on how well this step is executed. 3. Identify and calculate candidate moves. Finally, we arrive at the bottom layer of the burger. This step requires you to calculate candidate moves based on the threats you have identified. If there is nothing compelling, consider the following types of moves in this order: Checks, captures, threats. ______________________________________________________ I cannot over-emphasise the need for these steps to be taken sequentially. By skipping steps 1 and 2, you will likely miss what the position is telling you and hence have difficulty in finding candidate moves systematically. In this chapter, I will present a total of 30 puzzles divided into 8 thematic sets for you to practice The Burger Technique. The theme of each set can be found in the “informational” text page. For your easy reference, the three steps are presented at the start of each puzzle. To guide you along, I may insert prompting questions and hints. You may toggle the ‘eye’ icon to hide or reveal them. The answers and explanations of each move will only be revealed after the puzzle is completed. The Burger Technique is probably new to you and so it is perfectly normal to start slow. Hence, before we move on, I would like to give a word of advice. The emphasis should be on following through the three steps conscientiously rather than hastily. It may be tempting to skip the steps but I assure you that the results will be worth it if you persist. After all, this technique serves as the bedrock on which we will build to solve more complex positions in subsequent chapters. In time to come, you will develop a clearer thought process and calculate more accurately. Speed will take care of itself. Without further ado, let’s turn on the fire and start cooking. } 1. -- 
 *

