 { To find tactics that gain material effectively, you will first need to identify targets. There are many types of targets, but the first two common ones I’d like to introduce are hanging pieces and unprotected pieces. A hanging piece is one that has more attackers than defenders (e.g., 3 attackers and 1 defender, or 1 attacker and 0 defenders) . An unprotected piece is a piece that is not protected (i.e., 0 defenders) . It should also have 0 attackers. Why? The moment it has one or more attackers, it would be classified as a hanging piece instead. It is abundantly clear why hanging pieces can lead to material loss or gain, but what about unprotected pieces? Why are they considered to be targets if they aren’t attacked? The reason is that they are ALMOST hanging. If an attacker joins the party, the unprotected piece becomes a hanging piece, and is ripe for the taking. In this example, the d5 Knight and g5 Knight are unprotected. This allows White to add an attacker to both pieces and win one of them. } 1. { -KEY- } Re5 { The unprotected pieces are now hanging! } Ne6 2. Rxd5 { And White wins a piece. } 
 *

