 { The second approach is to identify unprotected pieces with the greatest number of attackers and defenders (e.g., A piece with 3 attackers and 3 defenders is more likely to fall than a piece with only 1 attacker and 1 defender) . When there are many attackers/defenders involved, you have more options in your play. With more attackers, you can consider different ways to capture the piece. By calculating various move orders, there is a possibility you will find a line that leads to a favourable material trade. Moreover, with more defenders, there is greater chance for any one of them to be forced out of its defensive duty. Once a defender is removed, you would have effectively turned the unprotected piece into a hanging piece. Let me give you an example. In this position, Black has two unprotected pieces – The b4 Knight (0 attackers and 0 defenders) and f3 Rook (1 attacker and 1 defender) . Between the two pieces, which is more vulnerable? To win the b4 Knight, we only have one option – add an attacker. This is because there are no defenders to speak of. However, to win the f3 Rook, we have two options – either add an attacker or remove a defender. This extra option turns out to be godsend. In this position, we can forcibly block the defender with its own King. } 1. { -KEY- } Re5+ Kf7 2. Kxf3 { To summarise the last few pages: The first two tactical motifs to look out for in step 2 of the Burger Technique are: 1. Hanging pieces (Pieces with more attackers than defenders) and 2. Unprotected pieces (Pieces with the same number of attackers as defenders) If you spot many unprotected pieces but cannot find any tactics, concentrate on the unprotected pieces that are most vulnerable. These pieces are exposed on many open lines and/or will have a high number of attackers/defenders. } 
 *

