 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces 3. Calculate candidate moves. Starting with step 1, we can see that material is equal with an imbalance. White has a Knight for Black's light-squared Bishop. In step 2, we should identify basic existing/potential threats. What are the unprotected and hanging pieces on both sides? White's f4 Queen is unprotected, the c5 Rook is hanging, and Black's c7 Rook and b5 Bishop is unprotected. Among these hanging/unprotected pieces, which is most likely to fall? It is the c7 Rook and c5 Rook. This is because they both have more attackers/defenders than the unprotected f4 Queen and b5 Bishop. Keeping in mind that we have to aim at the c7 target while keeping an eye out for c5, we can proceed to step 3. Mentally identify a few candidate moves and pick the best. } 1. { -KEY- } Qf6+ { This is a good move to start calculating. Not only are we giving check, we are also harassing the e7 Queen, a key defender of c7. } Qxf6 { This move is forced. Otherwise, we will take play 2. Qxe7 followed by 3. Rxc8. } ( 1... Kg8 2. Qxe7 Rxe7 3. Rxc8 ) 
2. exf6+ { Unfortunately for Black, he must deal with this check and let the c7 Rook fall. } Kxf6 3. Rxc7 Rxc7 4. Rxc7 { And we have a tremendous material advantage } 
