 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities 3. Calculate candidate moves. Step 1: Black is up one pawn. Step 2: There are no hanging pieces, but White has three undefended spots - both of his bishops and the h2 pawn. Black's f7 pawn is also unprotected. There may possibly be some tension between the Rooks on the e-file with only one Bishop in the way. Black's dark squares are also very tender with White's h6 Bishop staring down at the King position. Step 3: We can begin to examine candidate moves. When you are ready, make the best move for Black. } 1... { -KEY- } Qh4 { Given the ideas generated from step 2, Qh4 presents itself. } 2. Rxe5 { g3 allows black to obtain his advantage very simply. Therefore, white's best try is to remove black's dark squared Bishop and hope that his Bishop pair is enough compensation. } ( 2. g3 Qxh6 ( 2. Rxe5 Qxc4 $4 3. Rxe5 { Black has to deal with the threat of Qf6 } ) 
 ) 
 ( 2. Bd5 Qxh2 3. Kf1 Qh1 4. Ke2 Bf4 ) 
