 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities 3. Calculate candidate moves. Step 1: Black is down a Rook for only one pawn. Step 2: White's e2 Knight and all of Black's pieces are unprotected. Although White's c1 Rook and f4 pawn are technically protected, they remain as targets. This is because the e2 Knight, which is pinned and might be captured soon, is a poor defender to either of those squares. Black's potential threats are Rxe2+, Qxe2+, Qd4+, Qxc1, Be4, and Qxf4 while White's threat is to simply consolidate his overwhelming material advantage with Rhe1. Step 3: Based on what we have discussed, find a few candidate moves and pick the best. } 1... { -KEY- } Rxe2+ { Given that a lot of White's targets involve the e2 Knight, it makes sense to get rid of it with check. Black is temporarily only down an exchange for one pawn and has two major pieces on the second rank. On top of that, Be4 is a strong threat. Therefore, White must accept this sacrifice. } 2. Qxe2 { White is now up a Rook and an exchange for only one pawn and Black must either win the two rooks, find a mating attack, or at least a perpetual check } ( 2. Kg3 Be4 ) 
 ( 2. Kf1 Bd3 ) 
Qxf4+ { Since we just eliminated the e2 Knight, which was such a key defender, we should consider exploiting the new weaknesses. The two moves to consider would be Qxf4+ and Qd4+. Since Qxf4+ continues to attack the c1 Rook while Qd4+ does not, we should consider Qxf4+ first. } 3. Qf3 { This move is obviously forced. Since c1 is unprotected, the White King cannot step on the first rank. } ( 3. Ke1 Qxc1 4. Kf2 Qxh1 ) 
Qd2+ { This move again continues to threaten the c1 Rook, preventing the King from retreating to the first rank } 4. Kg3 Qg5+ 5. Kh3 { The only other square for the King to run to } ( 5. Kf2 Qd2 ) 
 ( 5. Qg4 { can be met with a familiar pattern } Qe3 6. Qf3 Qg5 ) 
Qh6+ ( 5... Bf5 6. g4 Qh5 7. Kg3 ) 
6. Kg4 Qg5+ 7. Kh3 { You were, and still are, down a significant amount of material. Hence, you can consider a draw to be a good result. If you were to find this position in a real game with increment, you should first ensure that all the lines after 1...Rxe2 lead to a quick draw or a decisive advantage. Afterwards, knowing that you would have secured at least a draw, you can spend the remainder of your time looking for a win, and only play out the draw at the last minute when you are unable. } 
