 { Amateur players miss countless tactics because they reject moves too hastily. A common piece of advice most coaches often dispense is to simply think deeper. However, it is impossible to work through every move exhaustively. For one, this is an overly demanding mental feat. It would also take too much time. Let me introduce a practical guide on how much you should analyse a given variation. Before deciding to reject any move, consider at least one-and-a-half moves ahead and carry out an evaluation of the static elements. This refers to king safety, quantity of material, and quality of material. If you think the resulting position is losing, confirm that it indeed cannot be salvaged with any desperate attempts. You can then safely reject the move. If you think you are winning, do not be conceited. You should certainly look through the variation a second time to check that your opponent does not have any defensive resources. You can then play out the win. However, if you think the resulting position is close to equal or unclear, you should continue to look for checks, captures and threats. For as long as these resources are available, continue to explore them until they are exhausted. At that point, conduct another evaluation of the static elements to determine if the initial move is good. I will present a few more puzzles to help you familiarise with this technique. Go through the drill conscientiously. Remember, never rush to reject or play a move. } 1. -- 
 *

