 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities 3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the Burger Technique from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it. Step 1: Material is equal with no imbalance. Step 2: All pieces and pawns are unprotected except for the Black c8 Rook. However, since its only defender, the e6 Bishop, is under attack, the Rook remains a target. White's potential threats are Rxe6+ and Rg7+. On top of that, the b6 pawn is far advanced and dangerous. Step 3: Find a few candidate moves based on the ideas we have discussed and pick the best. If no ideas come to mind, consider checks and captures first. } 57. { -KEY- } Rxe6+ { This should be the first move to consider, because it is the most forcing. Some players might reject this move immediately because it is losing an exchange in a very simplified position. However, this is premature because they have only looked half a move ahead. We should play out two more half moves before dismissing the move as a bad one. } Kxe6 58. Bg4+ { Getting the material back with interest. } Ke7 59. Bxc8 { This is a rather simple tactic. The takeaway is that rejecting your candidate moves without considering any possible continuations is a sure way to miss tactics. } 
