 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities 3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the Burger Technique from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it. Step 1: White is up one pawn and has a Knight for Black's light-squared Bishop. Step 2: White's Queen is hanging and the rest of his pieces are unprotected. On the other hand, Black only has his f2 Rook and f7 Bishop unprotected. White has chances for a good King hunt because almost his entire army is surrounding the enemy King. Step 3: Find a few candidate moves and pick the best. Be sure to pay special attention to the checks, especially given that Black's King position is suspect. } 1. { -KEY- } Ng6+ { This move is one of three possible checks. It is important to follow the guideline of considering at least one-and-a-half moves ahead before rejecting the other moves } ( 1. Rxh7 Kxh7 { Since we are down a significant amount of material and cannot even find any desperate moves, we can terminate our analysis here. } ) 
 ( 1. Rg8 Rxg8 2. Ng6 { We have now calculated one-and-a-half moves into the line and have to decide whether to continue calculating or to reject this line. Since we were able to play a very forcing check and might have the h-file opened up, it is worth further analysis. } Bxg6 3. hxg6 Rxg6 { We are losing quite badly. However, we should leave no stone unturned and explore the only check that we have in this position. } 4. Rxh7 Kxh7 { With only a Queen left and no good checks, we can safely reject 1. Rg8 } ) 
Bxg6 { This move is virtually forced since 1...hxg6 leads to mate } ( 1... hxg6 2. hxg6 Rh2 3. Rxh2 ) 
2. hxg6 { For similar reasons to what we discussed in the earlier lines, 2. Rxh7+ and 2. Rg8+ do not work. } ( 2. Rxh7 Bxh7 ) 
 ( 2. Rg8 Rxg8 ) 
Rf1+ { Again, this move is forced because of the threat of Rhxh7# } 3. Rxf1 ( 3. Kc2 Rxh1 { Looking half a move deeper, we realise that there are no Queen moves that can avoid the Queen trade and stop 4...Qxf6, removing any chances of an attack from White } ) 
dxc3 { It is a pity that many weaker players stop their calculations here, concluding that White is down too much material. However, since there are still a few reasonable checks to be played out, this position is worth further analysis. } 4. Rxh7+ Kg8 5. Rg7+ Kh8 6. Rh1# { This is a relatively simple mating combination since all moves come with check. Yet, many players terminate their analyses prematurely. Hence, for as long as you are able to find reasonable checks, you should continue to explore the line. } 
