Step 1: Material is equal, with both sides having opposite-coloured bishops. Step 2: Both of Black's rooks and White's d4 bishop are unprotected. White's d1 Rook is also a potential target with Black's Rook at d8 placed opposite it. White's main threat is to create a discovered attack on the d8 Rook by moving his d4 bishop away with tempo. However, with all the Black pieces placed on light squares, this seems unlikely. Black's threats are Rxd4 and Bc4+. Additionally, there is an outside chance of a back rank mate, given that e2 is covered right now. Step 3: With the help of what we have discussed in step 2, find a few candidate moves and pick the best. } 23... { -KEY- } Bc4+ { This is the first candidate move that we should consider because it is a check and one of our potential threats. It is also perfectly alright if you decided to look at 1...Rxf2 first, as long as you reject it only after looking through one-and-a half moves. } ( 23... Rxf2 24. Bxf2 { Black is down a lot of material and there are no suitable desperate moves. Hence, 1...Rxf2 can be rejected } ) 
