 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities d. Trappable pieces 3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the Burger Technique from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it. Step 1: White is down a pawn. Step 2: The unprotected pieces are White's a1 Rook, and Black's c5 Bishop and b2 Queen. Also, White's c3 Knight is hanging. All the potential threats are based around the unprotected pieces. Although it is not obvious, Black's b2 Queen counts as a trappable piece. For such a mobile piece, it strangely does not have many squares to run to. Its safe squares are b2, c3, a3, b4, and b6. Step 3: Keeping the b2 Queen's escape squares in mind, find a move to eliminate as many of them as possible, preferably with tempo. } 1. { -KEY- } Ndb5 { In a single move, White has taken away three of the Queen's safe squares, leaving it with only b2 and b4. This means that Rb1 on the next move will trap the Queen. Furthermore, White is threatening Bxc5 and Nc7+. With so many threats, Black's only chance is a counterattack. It is not possible to parry all these threats through passive moves. } Bb4 { This directly avoids White's threat of Bxc5. On top of that, White cannot play Nc7+ or Rb1 immediately because of Bxc3+. However, this means that White simply has to defend c3 and Black will still have to deal with these dual threats. } 2. Bd2 ( 2. Bd4 { works as well } ) 
