 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities d. Trappable pieces e. Pinned, pinnable, forkable, and skewerable pieces 3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the Burger Technique from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it. 
Step 1: Material is equal. Step 2: The unprotected pieces are Black's h3 Bishop, and White's h4 Knight and e4 Bishop. The e7 Queen is a target since it is placed opposite the e1 Rook. Conversely, even though the e1 Rook is overprotected with two defenders and no attackers, it remains a target. This is because Black has two X-ray attackers, the e6 Rook and e7 Queen. Thanks to the h3 Bishop, White also has a weak back rank. Therefore, the e4 Bishop is a pinnable piece. Step 3: Bearing all this in mind, the first move to consider should be quite clear. } 1... { -KEY- } Re8 { Pinning the Bishop and attacking it one more time. There is no escape from the dual threats of ...Rxe4 and ...f5 } 2. f3 f5 { As you can see, by identifying the e4 Bishop as a pinnable piece, we can easily find the best move in the position. } ( 2... f5 2. Bd3 Bd4 { Bringing another attacker into the game and pushing White's king to the corner. } 4. Kh1 { Now, White has back rank mate issues and we can take on e1. } Rxe1 $1 5. Rxe1 Qxe1 6. Bxe1 Rxe1 7. Bf1 Rxf1 ) 

