 { 1. Count the material and identify any material imbalance 2. Identify existing/potential threats a. Unprotected/hanging pieces b. X-Ray attacks c. Mating possibilities d. Trappable pieces e. Pinned, pinnable, forkable, and skewerable pieces f. Critical lines and squares 3. Calculate candidate moves and reject poor moves by repeating the Burger Technique from the opponent’s perspective. Be sure to calculate at least one and a half moves ahead before deciding to reject it. 
Step 1: White has a Knight for Black's pawn. Step 2: Only Black's g4 Rook is unprotected. This position does not seem to have any tactics. However, given that Black has doubled rooks on the file adjacent to the enemy monarch, the g-file is a critical line. Additionally, the g2 and g3 squares are critical because they are unprotected. Step 3: Recognising this new type of tactical idea, find a few candidate moves and pick the best. } 1... { -KEY- } Qg6 { Creating a deadly threat of Rg2! White has three ways to stop it. Unfortunately for White, it is easy to refute all of them using the g3 and g2 squares. } 2. Rg1 ( 2. Qe2 Rg3 { with an unstoppable threat of Rxh3+, where Kxh3 can be met with Qg3# } 3. Kh1 Rxh3 { Black has a deadly attack while up material. } 4. Rh2 Rxh2 5. Qxh2 Qg4 6. b5 h3 { White cannot keep the back rank, g2 square, and f3 square defended all at once. } ) 
 ( 2. Qf3 Rg3 { And White cannot deal with the dual threats of Rxh3+ and Rg2+ } ) 
Rxg1 3. Qxg1 ( 3. Nxg1 Qg3 4. Kh1 Qxf2 { Winning a Rook } ) 
Qg3+ 4. Qxg3 hxg3+ 5. Kg2 gxf2+ { In this puzzle, the tactic can only be solved with the help of the key squares, g2 and g3, and the key g-file. } 
