 { Congratulations! You have completed the first chapter of Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players. As you may have noticed, while the Burger Technique can yield very high accuracy, it appears to take quite a while to execute. So how can we apply it to real faster games (say, rapid/blitz time control) ? The short answer is, it will happen naturally with enough practice on puzzles. Every time you use the Burger Technique, you are training yourself to spot unprotected pieces faster and faster. Your judgment on critical squares and lines will also improve. Seeing X-rays will start to become second nature. At which point, when a tactical motif (such as an unprotected piece) presents itself in real games, your instincts will kick in as you stop there to look for the kill. As an example, I have chosen a famous game between Larry Christiansen vs Anatoly Karpov. Have a look at the game up till move 10…Nh5. } 1. { -KEY- } d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. a3 Ba6 5. Qc2 Bb7 6. Nc3 c5 7. e4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. Nxc6 Bxc6 10. Bf4 Nh5 { At this point, I am sure Christiansen, a strong grandmaster, immediately noticed that the Knight on h5 was vulnerable as it had 0 attackers and 0 defenders. And he certainly didn’t need to use the Burger Technique EVERY move to realise that ! It was just something that came to him naturally from decades of tactical training. But since there was no way to immediately capitalise on it, he retreated his Bishop to e3. } 11. Be3 Bd6 { Again, once Karpov left another piece unprotected, his d6 Bishop, it was intuitive for Christiansen to look for a way to exploit Black’s two unprotected pieces. Hence, he arrived at the powerful fork, Qd1! } 12. Qd1 { To wrap things up, in the next few chapters, we will be building on what we have learnt to improve our efficiency and speed. Now that you have memorised the three steps of the technique, it is no longer necessary for me to repeat it at the start of all commentaries for the rest of the course. However, you should continue to mentally go through the drill in tandem with new techniques I will introduce along the way. } 
 *
